b.add(S5, 317, "scenario", "A patient has sudden chest pain, shock and a short low-pitched early diastolic murmur after an aortic dissection. Which management does page 317 prioritise?", "Immediate surgery after bridging with NTG/nitroprusside/inotropes", ["Routine yearly echocardiography", "Balloon-expandable TAVR only", "Oral torsemide 5–10 mg as definitive therapy"], "Aortic dissection is an acute-AR cause, the features fit acute LVF/shock and treatment is immediate surgery with NTG, nitroprusside and inotropes while awaiting it.")
b.add(S5, 317, "truefalse", "TRUE or FALSE about acute versus chronic AR.", "True — chronic compensated AR can retain a normal EF, while acute AR causes pulmonary edema because there is no time to adapt", ["True — acute AR has eccentric hypertrophy and chronic AR always causes immediate shock", "False — chronic AR is treated only with emergency surgery", "False — acute AR has a long high-pitched murmur and loud S1"], "The p313 chronic branch has normal/increased EF with eccentric hypertrophy; acute AR has no adaptation, raised LVEDP/LAP and edema, and its EDM is short and low pitched with soft S1.")
b.add(S5, 317, "oddoneout", "Pick the ODD ONE OUT — one item is not an acute-AR cause printed on page 317.", "Bicuspid aortic valve with NOTCH-1 mutation", ["Aortic dissection", "Infective endocarditis", "Rupture of the sinus of Valsalva"], "The acute-AR causes are dissection, infective endocarditis, ruptured sinus of Valsalva and trauma; BAV/NOTCH-1 belongs to the AS section.")

b.guide(S1, "AR is first divided into acute and chronic disease (p313). Chronic leaflet causes are rheumatic (MS + AR or AR + AS), congenital quadricuspid valves, Takayasu arteritis and ankylosing spondylitis; root causes are syphilis (m/c), Marfan and Ehlers–Danlos syndromes, with rheumatological Takayasu, Behcet, Cogan (aortitis + interstitial keratitis + vestibulitis) and IgG4-related large-vessel vasculitis.\nMild–moderate AR is symptom-free ± palpitations, while severe disease is symptomatic from LV failure. The diastolic gradient is drawn between LV 10 mmHg and aorta 80 mmHg.\nAn incompetent leaflet fails to close, blood leaks from aorta to LV, and eccentric hypertrophy with dilatation compensates; EF = (EDV − ESV)/EDV remains normal/increased and LVEDP is normal.")
b.guide(S2, "Decompensated AR moves from LVF to normal/low EF, markedly raised ESV with S3 and markedly raised LVED → raised LAP → dyspnea (p314). Preload rises with blood from LA/LV and aorta/LV, afterload rises with systolic BP/stretch/Frank–Starling contractility, DBP falls as blood regurgitates (it may touch zero) and pulse pressure is wide.\nWide PP produces Hill's >20 mmHg leg–arm difference (surest sign) and Lincoln's prominent popliteal pulsations. Nocturnal angina follows sleeping bradycardia → prolonged diastole → worse coronary steal, while vasoconstriction raises aortic pressure and gradient; hence strict BP control, and exercise tachycardia shortens diastole.\nThe comparison table gives AS angina +++ first/nocturnal AR +, AS syncope +/AR −, AS dyspnea ++/AR + with LVF and AS palpitations −/AR +. High-volume pulse is increased LV blood → increased EF → increased volume; water-hammer/collapsing pulse is that pulse with routes for exit.")
b.guide(S3, "Collapsing pulses are also placed in regurgitation, aorta–pulmonary window and PDA (p315). Collapsing AR has a sharp upstroke/downstroke with ill-sustained apex and high volume; pseudocollapsing MR has normal volume.\nBisferiens has P (percussion) and T (tidal) systolic peaks: P > T means HCM, while P < T or P = T means severe AR ± mild–moderate AS. BP is wide (Hill measured at mid-thigh prone), the apex is hyperdynamic down-and-out and the precordium rocks.\nThe heart-sound table gives soft S1 from premature closure, root disease loud A2 ± reverse split (P2–A2), valve disease soft A2, S3 only in failure and no S4. The high-pitched soft blowing early diastolic decrescendo murmur is at the neo-aortic/3rd left ICS, radiates to axilla (Cole–Cecil), is best heard with the diaphragm at end-expiration while sitting/leaning forward, and leaflet radiation is Key–Hodgkin murmur.")
b.guide(S4, "The p316 tracing is an early diastolic decrescendo murmur after S2. Associated murmurs are an ESM at the base and a low-pitched mid-diastolic Austin Flint murmur from anterior mitral leaflet displacement by the regurgitant jet.\nThe 16 peripheral signs, in printed order, are Corrigan (carotid/supraclavicular pulsations), Alfred de Musset (head nodding), Landolfi (pupil-size change), Müller (uvula), Becker (retinal artery), Rosenbach (liver), Gerhardt (spleen), Quincke (digital capillaries), Lighthouse (forehead blanching/flushing), Traube ('pistol shot' femoral sound), Duroziez (to-and-fro femoral murmur with light bell compression), Hill (>20 mmHg leg–arm systolic difference), water-hammer (abruptly collapsing high-volume pulse), Mayne (>15 mmHg DBP fall with arm raised), Lincoln (pulsatile popliteal) and Sherman (unexpected dorsalis pedis prominence after age 75 years).")
b.guide(S5, "Symptomatic chronic AR goes directly to aortic valve replacement; asymptomatic severe AR uses the 55–50 rule, EF ≤55% or LVEDD ≥50% (p317).\nAcute AR follows aortic dissection with chest pain, infective endocarditis, ruptured sinus of Valsalva with right-heart failure, or trauma. With no time to adapt, raised LVEDP and LAP >25 mmHg cause acute pulmonary edema and LV failure; the EDM is short and low pitched with soft S1 and LVF can produce cardiogenic shock and hypotension.\nImmediate surgery is definitive. While awaiting surgery, NTG, nitroprusside and inotropes are grouped together as lowering aortic pressure.")

if __name__ == "__main__":
    b.write()
